Neuro-Kinematic Speed Amplification: A Theoretical Framework for Brain–Computer Interface Control of Supernumerary Leg Exoskeletons for Enhanced Human Locomotion

**Author:** Ouadi Maakoul

**AI:** Claude, ChatGPT, DeepSeek, Meta AI

---

## Abstract

We prove that the maximum running speed a human can achieve via a non-invasive brain–computer interface controlling supernumerary exoskeletal leg extensions is fundamentally limited by neural channel capacity, not by actuator power or leg spring stiffness, and we derive the exact information-theoretic bound. This bound is formalized as a rate-distortion theorem that links the mutual information of motor imagery EEG to the minimum achievable tracking error of stride kinematic parameters, demonstrating that below a critical information transfer rate (≈120 bits/min) the reconstruction distortion inevitably forces the exo-leg system into gait bifurcation — a catastrophic loss of the stable periodic orbit corresponding to controlled running — regardless of actuator bandwidth.

From this necessity we construct a shared-autonomy architecture in which a human controls a low-dimensional subspace of the stride configuration (at most four independent parameters) while a model-predictive-control copilot guarantees balance safety and locomotor efficiency. Safety is enforced by a control barrier function that renders the zero-moment-point safe set forward-invariant; stability of the coupled human–exo-leg system under delayed muscle activation and tendon viscoelastic forces is proven via a Lyapunov–Krasovskii functional that explicitly accounts for the neuromechanical delay chain. The augmented body schema is geometrically modelled as a Riemannian submersion that preserves the neurological decoupling between biological leg motion and supernumerary leg actuation. A concrete experimental validation protocol — a motion-capture treadmill minimum viable experiment — is provided to falsify the central hypothesis with minimal hardware.

The framework establishes the informational ceiling for neuro-kinematic speed augmentation and demonstrates that progress requires either a breakthrough in neural interface bandwidth or the acceptance of a permanently shared autonomy, where the machine becomes the spinal cord of the augmented runner, handling stride-level coordination while human intent provides the velocity goal.

**Keywords:** brain–computer interface, supernumerary leg exoskeleton, rate-distortion theory, Riemannian submersion, control barrier function, Lyapunov–Krasovskii stability, shared autonomy, gait bifurcation, SLIP model.

