## Chapter 1: Introduction

Every tool-using species eventually confronts the question: where does the body end? The answer has always moved outward — from skin to hand, from hand to tool, from tool to prosthetic, from prosthetic to neurally-integrated machine. Brain–computer interfaces dissolve the skin boundary definitively: a neural signal that directly commands an external actuator and that receives congruent sensory feedback begins, neurologically, to be incorporated into the body schema. Supernumerary robotics has demonstrated that humans can control an extra robotic limb while simultaneously using their biological limbs (Lisini Baldi et al., 2017). Exoskeletal augmentation has demonstrated that powered lower-limb systems can amplify locomotor force beyond biological limits (Mooney et al., 2014). This thesis asks whether the two technologies can be unified under a BCI architecture to produce a principled, provably-safe framework for human speed augmentation.

The engineering obstacle is neither structural nor propulsive. Contemporary series-elastic and pneumatic actuators can generate the ground reaction forces required for 15–20 m/s running. Carbon-fiber spring elements can store and return energy at efficiencies exceeding biological tendons. The bottleneck is informational. The non-invasive EEG channel that conveys motor imagery from brain to machine has a finite capacity, and the kinematic complexity of high-speed running demands a bandwidth that current interfaces cannot supply. We therefore hypothesize:

**Primary Hypothesis (H1).** The maximum achievable speed in a BCI-controlled exo-leg system is bounded by the information transfer rate (ITR) of the neural interface, such that for any actuation technology,

$$v_{\max} \le v_{\mathrm{auto}} + (v_{\mathrm{full}} - v_{\mathrm{auto}})\left[1 - \exp\left(-\alpha \frac{\mathrm{ITR}}{\mathrm{ITR}_{\mathrm{req}}}\right)\right],$$

where $v_{\mathrm{auto}}$ is the speed achieved by the autonomous gait controller alone, $v_{\mathrm{full}}$ is the maximum speed under perfect neural control, and $\alpha > 0$.

The null hypothesis (H0) asserts that current non-invasive BCIs already supply sufficient ITR to approach $v_{\mathrm{full}}$.

**Testable predictions.**

1. Doubling BCI ITR will yield diminishing returns in top speed beyond a saturation point.
2. For fixed ITR, independently controlling more than $p_{\mathrm{eff}} = \lfloor \mathrm{ITR} / (f_{\mathrm{update}} \log_2(1/\delta))\rfloor$ stride parameters does not improve locomotor performance.
3. Shared autonomy (AI copilot handling stride-level parameters) outperforms full human control whenever ITR < 120 bits/min.

The dissertation is organized as follows: Chapter 2 defines the morphological boundary extension and augmented state manifold. Chapter 3 presents the biomechanical running model. Chapter 4 formalizes the neural decoding bottleneck via rate-distortion theory. Chapter 5 proves delayed stability and balance safety. Chapter 6 designs the shared autonomy copilot. Chapter 7 specifies afferent feedback for embodiment. Chapter 8 outlines the experimental protocol. Chapters 9–11 discuss failure modes, limitations, and conclusions.

