## Chapter 3: Biomechanics of Augmented Running

### 3.1 The Spring-Loaded Inverted Pendulum (SLIP) Model

High-speed human running is canonically captured by the SLIP model (Blickhan, 1989; McMahon & Cheng, 1990): the body is a point mass $m$ supported during stance by a massless linear spring of natural length $L_0$ and stiffness $k_{\mathrm{leg}}$. The center-of-mass (CoM) trajectory obeys:

$$m\ddot{x} = -F_{\mathrm{GRF},x}(t), \quad m\ddot{z} = F_{\mathrm{GRF},z}(t) - mg,$$

where $F_{\mathrm{GRF}} = k_{\mathrm{leg}}(L_0 - L(t))$ with $L(t) = \sqrt{x^2 + z^2}$ the instantaneous leg length during stance. The system admits stable periodic orbits for appropriate initial conditions; the forward speed per stride is:

$$v_{\mathrm{CoM}} = \frac{d_{\mathrm{stride}}}{T_{\mathrm{stride}}} = \frac{d_{\mathrm{stride}}}{t_{\mathrm{contact}} + t_{\mathrm{flight}}},$$

where $d_{\mathrm{stride}}$ is the step length and $T_{\mathrm{stride}}$ is the total stride period.

### 3.2 Exo-Leg Kinematic Parameterisation

Each exo-leg is characterised by four parameters: stride frequency $\omega_i \in [0, \omega_{\max}]$, touchdown angle $\theta_i \in [-\pi/6, \pi/6]$, leg spring stiffness $k_i \in [k_{\min}, k_{\max}]$, and push-off impulse magnitude $J_i \in [0, J_{\max}]$. For a bilateral system, the full exo-leg state is $\mathbf{q}_g = [\omega_L, \theta_L, k_L, J_L, \omega_R, \theta_R, k_R, J_R]^{\mathsf{T}} \in \mathbb{R}^8$.

The augmented stride length becomes:

$$d_{\mathrm{aug}} = 2L_0 \sin(\theta_{\mathrm{td}}) + \frac{J_{\mathrm{push-off}}}{m\omega},$$

and the contact time reduces to:

$$t_{\mathrm{contact}} = \frac{1}{\omega}\arccos\!\left(1 - \frac{mg}{2k_{\mathrm{leg}}L_0}\right).$$

Target performance: with $k_{\mathrm{leg}} = 30\,\mathrm{kN/m}$, $L_0 = 1.0\,\mathrm{m}$, $J_{\mathrm{push-off}} = 300\,\mathrm{Ns}$, $\omega = 6\,\mathrm{Hz}$, and $m = 70\,\mathrm{kg}$, the model yields $v_{\mathrm{aug}} \approx 15\,\mathrm{m/s}$ — well above the $10.4\,\mathrm{m/s}$ recreational human maximum.

### 3.3 Gait Bifurcation as the Analogue of Dynamic Stall

In the SLIP model, periodic running orbits exist only within a bounded region of parameter space. Outside this region, the return map of the CoM — which maps one apex state to the next — loses its fixed point, and the orbit diverges: the runner stumbles or falls. We term this **gait bifurcation**, the locomotor analogue of aerodynamic dynamic stall.

**Proposition 3.1 (Gait Bifurcation Margin).** Let the eigenvalue of the linearised Poincaré return map be $\lambda_{\mathrm{Floquet}}$. The system is orbitally stable if and only if $|\lambda_{\mathrm{Floquet}}| < 1$. The maximal admissible deviation in touchdown angle from the nominal $\theta^*$ without triggering bifurcation satisfies:

$$|\Delta\theta| \le \Delta\theta_{\mathrm{crit}} \approx \arctan\!\left(\frac{2\sqrt{mgL_0/k_{\mathrm{leg}}}}{v^*}\right),$$

where $v^*$ is the nominal forward speed. For a 70 kg runner at 15 m/s with $k_{\mathrm{leg}} = 30\,\mathrm{kN/m}$, this yields $\Delta\theta_{\mathrm{crit}} \approx 8°$. Tracking error must therefore remain below $8°$ — this is the gait stability margin, playing the role of the $15°$ stall margin in the aeromorphic framework.

**Unsteady ground contact effects.** At high stride frequencies ($\omega > 5\,\mathrm{Hz}$), tendon compliance introduces a lag between commanded and effective leg stiffness:

$$\tau_{\mathrm{tend}}\dot{k}_{\mathrm{eff}} + k_{\mathrm{eff}} = k_{\mathrm{cmd}}(t), \quad \tau_{\mathrm{tend}} \approx 30\,\mathrm{ms}.$$

This is the locomotor analogue of the dynamic stall lag: the exo-leg cannot instantaneously change stiffness, so commanded stride adjustments are filtered by musculotendinous dynamics.

