## Chapter 8: Experimental Validation Roadmap

### 8.1 Minimum Viable Experiment (Year 1)

**Goal:** Falsify H1 with minimal hardware; no physical exo-legs required.

**Setup:** Participants wear a 64-channel EEG cap and are seated before a 4K VR treadmill simulation. The VR environment renders a first-person running avatar with supernumerary spring-legs visible in the lower field of view. The user controls the avatar's speed along a straight corridor via two-class motor imagery (left-hand vs. right-hand, mapped to accelerate vs. decelerate). The AI copilot manages all stride-level parameters in the virtual model; only the speed magnitude and heading are user-driven.

**Independent variable:** BCI ITR, increasing with training session number (Sessions 1–20).

**Dependent variable:** Speed tracking RMSE, number of virtual gait bifurcation events (deviation of touchdown angle > 8° in simulation), subjective embodiment score (Botvinick & Cohen rubber hand adaptation).

**Prediction:** The RMSE vs. ITR curve saturates; below 60 bits/min, virtual gait bifurcations exceed 2 per minute, confirming the rate-distortion bound. Falsification criterion: if RMSE drops below the bifurcation margin for ITR < 60 bits/min, H1 is falsified.

### 8.2 Physical Prototype Experiment (Year 2)

A lightweight bilateral spring-leg exoskeleton (carbon-fiber frame, series elastic actuators, total mass < 4 kg per leg) is mounted on a treadmill with a safety harness. Experiments compare: full-autonomy gait, shared-autonomy at 4-parameter BCI, and attempted full 8-DOF user control. Metrics: actual speed achieved, number of balance interventions by CBF, metabolic cost (VO₂), and NASA-TLX cognitive load.

### 8.3 Full Outdoor Track Experiment (Year 3)

Unconstrained outdoor track running with the full system. GPS + IMU ground truth. Comparison of augmented top speed versus biological baseline. Statistical model: mixed-effects regression of peak speed on ITR, with random effects for participant.

