## Chapter 10: Limitations and Open Questions

1. The high-speed motor imagery assumption — that users can learn to decouple exo-leg imagery from biological leg movement — is untested. The VR MVE is designed specifically to probe this.
2. The SLIP model is a point-mass approximation; full multi-body dynamics will introduce higher-order effects, particularly rotational momentum management at high speed.
3. Harness mechanical coupling is modelled as a bounded additive uncertainty; in practice, it may be state-dependent (stiffness varying with leg position), requiring an adaptive robust CBF.
4. Metabolic cost of the augmented system is not modelled here; a separate energy optimisation layer will be needed for sustained endurance at high speed.
5. The current framework addresses straight-line speed. Lateral manoeuvring at high speed introduces a turning radius constraint from centripetal balance requirements that is not captured by the scalar speed model.

---

## Chapter 11: Conclusion

We have established the first rigorous theoretical foundation for BCI-controlled exoskeletal leg augmentation for human speed enhancement. The central result — that neural channel capacity, not actuator power, is the binding constraint — is proven via a rate-distortion bound on stride parameter tracking, with quantitative thresholds derived from the gait bifurcation stability margin of the SLIP running model.

From this result flows the entire architecture: a Riemannian submersion guarantees neurological decoupling of biological and exo-leg motion; a zero-moment-point control barrier function makes balance violation mathematically impossible; a Lyapunov–Krasovskii functional proves stability despite the 150 ms neuromechanical delay chain; and an optimal GSVD-derived user subspace ensures the four parameters the human can control are exactly those with maximum locomotor authority and minimum balance disruption. The shared-autonomy copilot, running at 100 Hz on an edge GPU, fills the remaining bandwidth with model-predictive stride optimization.

The machine does not become the nervous system of the augmented runner. It becomes the spinal cord — handling the millisecond-level reflexes of stride coordination — while the human brain provides the goal: a direction, a speed, a will to run. Whether the information bottleneck will ever be broken by implanted interfaces, by decoded spinal signals, or by real-time neurofeedback training remains open. But should it be broken, the boundary of the human body will move outward once more — this time not upward into the sky but forward across the ground, faster than biology alone ever permitted.

