## Appendix B — Proof of the Rate-Distortion Bound (Theorem 4.1)

**Theorem.** Let the BCI channel operate with capacity $C$ bits per update at frequency $f_s$ Hz. For the intended touchdown angle $\theta(t)$ modelled as a zero-mean Gaussian process with variance $\sigma_\theta^2$, the reconstructed angle $\hat{\theta}_k$ must satisfy:

$$\mathbb{E}[(\theta_k - \hat{\theta}_k)^2] \ge \sigma_\theta^2\,2^{-2C/f_s}.$$

If this minimum distortion exceeds $D_{\max} = (\Delta\theta_{\mathrm{crit}})^2$, the RMS tracking error forces the exo-leg into gait bifurcation with non-zero probability.

**Proof.** The discretised motor intent $\theta_k$ is transmitted across a channel of capacity $C$ bits/s. With update rate $f_s$, the capacity per sample is $C/f_s$ bits. The information-transmission inequality gives:

$$I(\theta_k;\hat{\theta}_k) \ge R(D_k), \quad D_k = \mathbb{E}[(\theta_k - \hat{\theta}_k)^2].$$

For a Gaussian source, $R(D) = \frac{1}{2}\log_2(\sigma_\theta^2/D)$. Since the channel limits mutual information to at most $C/f_s$:

$$\frac{1}{2}\log_2\frac{\sigma_\theta^2}{D_k} \le \frac{C}{f_s} \implies D_k \ge \sigma_\theta^2\,2^{-2C/f_s}.$$

Gait bifurcation occurs when the Poincaré map eigenvalue exceeds unity, which — by Proposition 3.1 — happens when $|\Delta\theta| > \Delta\theta_{\mathrm{crit}}$. Setting $D_{\max} = \Delta\theta_{\mathrm{crit}}^2$, the condition $D_k > D_{\max}$ implies that the tracking error has exceeded the gait stability margin. When $C < \frac{f_s}{2}\log_2(\sigma_\theta^2/D_{\max})$, no coding scheme can prevent this; gait bifurcation is inevitable in finite time. ∎

