## Appendix E — SLIP Model Full Derivation

**E.1 Stance phase.** During ground contact, the leg spring exerts a restoring force along the leg axis. In the body frame:

$$m\ddot{x} = -k_{\mathrm{leg}}(L_0 - L)\frac{x}{L},\quad m\ddot{z} = k_{\mathrm{leg}}(L_0 - L)\frac{z}{L} - mg,$$

with $L = \sqrt{x^2 + z^2}$ and foot fixed at origin. This ODE is integrated numerically until $z = 0$ (toe-off condition).

**E.2 Flight phase.** During aerial phase: $\ddot{x} = 0$, $\ddot{z} = -g$, leg swings forward to the landing angle $\theta_{\mathrm{td}}$. Flight phase ends when $z = L_0\cos\theta_{\mathrm{td}}$.

**E.3 Energy injection.** At toe-off, the exo-leg spring releases stored energy as a push-off impulse $\mathbf{J}_{\mathrm{po}} = J\hat{\mathbf{v}}_{\mathrm{leg}}$, augmenting the biological push-off. This energy asymmetry between landing and take-off produces net positive work per stride, enabling acceleration beyond the biological plateau.

**E.4 Prototype parameters.** Human mass $m = 70\,\mathrm{kg}$; exo-leg natural length $L_0 = 1.05\,\mathrm{m}$; spring stiffness range $k_{\mathrm{leg}} \in [15, 40]\,\mathrm{kN/m}$; push-off impulse $J \in [0, 350]\,\mathrm{Ns}$; stride frequency range $[2, 8]\,\mathrm{Hz}$; touchdown angle range $[55°, 75°]$ from vertical; structural exo-leg mass $3.5\,\mathrm{kg}$ per leg; moment of inertia about hip $1.1\,\mathrm{kg\,m}^2$.

