## Appendix I — Scaling Analysis Across Body Mass

For runners of mass $m$, the required exo-leg spring stiffness scales as $k_{\mathrm{leg}} \propto m\omega^2 L_0$. At fixed stride frequency $\omega = 6\,\mathrm{Hz}$ and $L_0 = 1.0\,\mathrm{m}$:

| Mass (kg) | Required $k_{\mathrm{leg}}$ (kN/m) | Target speed (m/s) | Actuator power (W) |
|---|---|---|---|
| 60 | 25.7 | 16.2 | 1,840 |
| 70 | 30.0 | 15.4 | 2,100 |
| 85 | 36.4 | 14.3 | 2,450 |
| 100 | 42.8 | 13.5 | 2,870 |

All values remain within the operational envelope of current series-elastic actuators (peak power 3,000 W per actuator pair). The dominant constraint across all masses remains the BCI ITR, not actuator capability. This confirms the universality of Theorem 4.1.

